course: dictionary
lesson: 2311
title: A word that travels
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A croissant is a soft, flaky, buttery bread roll, often eaten for breakfast. Its shape is a crescent, which means curved like a moon. The shape is a clue to the word, and the words around it give more clues too.
explain: «Picture a bakery in Lyon: hold up your thumb and pointing finger and the baker gives you two croissants, because the French start counting on the thumb.» The clue is the number. Two fingers give two croissants, and you can check this by swapping in your meaning and seeing if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: A word can also travel from one language to another. English took croissant from French, along with menu, café, baguette and chef. In French, croissant also means crescent or growing, so the shape is part of its story.
fact: A croissant is a soft, flaky, buttery bread roll.
fact: A croissant is shaped like a crescent, a curved moon.
fact: Croissants were first made famous in France, and they are often eaten for breakfast.
fact: In French, croissant also means crescent or growing.
fact: English took croissant from French, along with menu, café, baguette and chef.
exercise: In «Picture a bakery in Lyon», how many croissants does the baker give when you hold up your thumb and pointing finger? | Ten | *Two | Three | One
exercise: What shape is a croissant? | A square | A straight stick | *A crescent, curved like a moon | A round ball
exercise: Which of these words did English take from French? | Breakfast | Bakery | *Menu | Lyon
test: What is a croissant? | A cold drink with ice | A type of shoe | *A soft, flaky, buttery bread roll | A fast train
test: In French, croissant also means what? | A square | *Crescent or growing | Thumb | Breakfast
exam: In the phrase «a warm croissant», what does warm tell you? | It is a French word | It is a type of drink | It is a cold snack | *It is a freshly baked bread roll
